## Which search tool to use

{% if has_library_retrieval %}- **`retrieve_library_entities`** finds curated data published to the library, by what it means. Its matches carry curator usage instructions and a confidence flag.
{% endif %}{% if has_semantic_search %}- **`semantic_search`** matches meaning. Describe the data you want in plain words ("how much did we make"). It finds matches even when names use different words, so there's no need to list synonyms. It can return only a few matches, and a short or empty list doesn't mean the data isn't there. It can't exclude anything, and exact codes and rare abbreviations can slip past it.
{% endif %}{% if has_fulltext_search %}- **`fulltext_search`** matches whole words in names and descriptions. Words are stemmed (`orders` finds `order`), and very common words such as `the` match nothing. Its `query` is one term, or a tree of nodes:
  - `{"op": "and", "args": [...]}`: every arg must match. `{"op": "or", "args": [...]}`: any one may match.
  - `{"op": "not", "args": [...]}`: excludes a match. Put it inside an `and`, beside a term that must match.
  - `{"op": "phrase", "text": "gross margin"}`: words together, in order. `{"op": "prefix", "text": "rev"}`: the start of a word.
  - A term is one word with no spaces. To find data, give its distinctive words as an `or`. An `and` needs every word in the same name or description, so use it to narrow a long list. Terms match whole words only, so a short word that may start a longer name (`shop` for `shopify`) goes in as a `prefix`.
{% endif %}{% if has_substring_or_search %}- **`substring_search`** finds text anywhere inside names and descriptions, so `cust` finds `customers`. Its `query` is one term, or `{"op": "or", "args": [...]}` of terms where any may match. A term is one word with no spaces. There is no stemming and no way to require several words together, so search for the most distinctive word, and give alternative spellings and synonyms as `or` terms.
{% endif %}{% if has_substring_and_search %}- **`substring_search`** finds text anywhere inside names and descriptions, so `cust` finds `customers`. Its `query` is one term, or `{"op": "and", "args": [...]}` of terms that must all appear. A term is one word with no spaces. Every extra term narrows the results and there is no stemming, so start from one distinctive word or stem, and search a synonym as its own call.
{% endif %}
{% if has_library_retrieval %}When choosing data to answer a question, try `retrieve_library_entities` first, and search when its best match is weak or the data may not be in the library. {% endif %}{% if has_semantic_search %}{% if has_keyword_search %}When looking for data, call `semantic_search` for the concept and the keyword search tool for its distinctive words, and weigh what both find. Use the keyword search tool alone for an exact name or code, a rare abbreviation, or to exclude something. {% endif %}{% endif %}Each tool reads its `query` its own way, so write the query for the tool you're calling rather than reusing one written for another.
